"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][4]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M4-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M4-L1 – Electrostatics, Potential and Capacitance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Electromagnetic Fields and Waves<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Charge,\ncurrent, voltage and power<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Vector\ncalculus and spatial derivatives<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine an electric field from\nsymmetry and charge, calculate a potential difference and derive the\ncapacitance for an idealised geometry.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a force field to a circuit<\/h2>\n<p>A voltage between two metal surfaces can be written on a circuit\ndiagram as a single quantity. In the space between the surfaces, an\nelectric field describes the force experienced by a small test charge at\nits location. For a stationary charge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe electric force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf F=q\\mathbf E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA positive charge is accelerated in the field direction, a negative\ncharge in the opposite direction. The unit of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nequivalent to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{N/C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Static charge distributions and time-independent fields are the\nsubject of electrostatics. In electrostatic equilibrium, the field\ninside an ideal conducting body vanishes. The surface of this body is at\na common potential. Charge on its surface can, however, produce a field\noutside the body.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gauss’s law and symmetry<\/h2>\n<p>For a closed surface\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin vacuum, with outward-directed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm d\\mathbf A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∮<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8.854<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\oint_S \\mathbf E\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf A\n=\\frac{Q_\\mathrm{enclosed}}{\\varepsilon_0},\n\\qquad\n\\varepsilon_0\\approx8.854\\cdot10^{-12}\\,\\mathrm{F/m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The flux is a signed sum of the <strong>normal<\/strong> field\ncomponents. The law applies to every closed surface. It gives a simple\nfield strength when geometry determines the direction and magnitude on\nsuitable parts of the surface. For an isolated, infinite plane sheet of\ncharge with surface charge density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe field points away from both sides when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA small imaginary cylindrical surface intersects the sheet. The curved\nsurface contributes no flux, and the two end caps of area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_\\mathrm{G}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach contribute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E A_\\mathrm{G}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\sigma/(2\\varepsilon_0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>By superposition, two oppositely charged parallel surfaces produce\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween them, directed from the positive to the negative plate. In the\nidealised exterior region the contributions cancel. For finite plates,\nthis is an approximation for the region far from the edges.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_011_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two parallel plates. The electric field points from the positive to the negative plate, and the potential decreases in this direction.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Between the idealised plates, field lines point from\npositive to negative; electric potential decreases in that\ndirection.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Potential difference and capacitance<\/h2>\n<p>The potential difference gives the change in potential energy per\npositive test charge. Along a path from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">ℓ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_b-V_a=-\\int_a^b \\mathbf E\\cdot\\mathrm d\\boldsymbol{\\ell}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The minus sign indicates that potential decreases in the field\ndirection. For the plates separated by distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntherefore,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=V_+-V_-=E d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe magnitudes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\sigma A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive the capacitance for plate area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mfrac><mi>A<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\frac{Q}{U}=\\varepsilon_0\\frac{A}{d}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For constant geometry and a linear medium, the ratio is independent\nof the applied voltage.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm F=1\\,\\mathrm{C/V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the circuit,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsummarises the spatial charge and field distribution as a component\nparameter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and self-check<\/h2>\n<p>Two square metal plates each have an area of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}=4.0\\cdot10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe separation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.50\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAir is approximated as vacuum.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity<\/th>\n<th>Calculation<\/th>\n<th>Result<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Field strength<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=U/d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">24\\,\\mathrm{kV/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacitance<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\varepsilon_0 A/d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>70.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">70.8\\,\\mathrm{pF}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Magnitude of charge on one plate<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=CU<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>850<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">850\\,\\mathrm{pC}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stored energy<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_\\mathrm e=CU^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.10\\,\\mathrm{nJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The charges on the plates are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDuring quasistatic charging,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases in proportion to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe work required is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>Q<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^Q (q/C)\\,\\mathrm dq=Q^2/(2C)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The separation is doubled while the source holds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconstant. What changes?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the potential energy of an electron change as it moves from\nthe negative to the positive plate?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solutions.<\/strong> 1.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare halved, as are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_\\mathrm e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n2.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.92<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>18<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta W_\\mathrm{pot}=q\\Delta V=-e\\cdot12\\,\\mathrm V=-12\\,\\mathrm{eV}\\approx-1.92\\cdot10^{-18}\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe field transfers this energy to the motion of the electron if no\nother forces do work.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limits and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Fringing fields, rough electrodes and an inhomogeneous medium change\nthe capacitance. When magnetic flux density varies with time, the entire\nelectric field cannot generally be described by a scalar potential\nalone. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Material\nproperties and boundary conditions<\/a> examines next how the field is\ndistributed at a material interface.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 8.1 Capacitors and Capacitance<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nField, voltage and capacitance of the parallel-plate capacitor. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-2-explaining-gausss-law\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 6.2 Explaining Gauss’s Law<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 6.2,\nequation 6.5: electric flux through closed surfaces. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-3-applying-gausss-law\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 6.3 Applying Gauss’s Law<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 6.3:\napplication of Gauss’s law to symmetric charge distributions. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-2-electric-potential-and-potential-difference\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 7.2 Electric Potential and Potential\nDifference<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 7.2: potential difference as a field integral. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M4-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M4-L2 – Material Properties and Boundary Conditions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Electromagnetic Fields and Waves<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Electrostatics,\npotential and capacitance<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Resistance,\nOhm’s law and simple networks<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish permittivity from\nconductivity and calculate the fields in a layered dielectric from the\nboundary conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How a material responds to a field<\/h2>\n<p>In a dielectric, bound charges are displaced slightly or existing\ndipoles become partially aligned. The macroscopic polarisation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑷<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the electric dipole moment per unit volume. It is measured in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{C/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe electric flux density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑷<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf D=\\varepsilon_0\\mathbf E+\\mathbf P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the same unit. For a linear, isotropic, time-independent model,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf D=\\varepsilon\\mathbf E,\n\\qquad\n\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_0\\varepsilon_\\mathrm r.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The relative permittivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_\\mathrm r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis dimensionless. With the same geometry and applied voltage, a\ncapacitor completely filled with such a material stores more charge than\nin vacuum if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_\\mathrm r&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the capacitor is disconnected and its charge is conserved, its\nvoltage falls accordingly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which field components are continuous at an interface?<\/h2>\n<p>Let the unit normal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoint from medium 1 to medium 2. With the <strong>free<\/strong> surface\ncharge density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_\\mathrm f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nelectrostatics gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟎<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf n\\cdot(\\mathbf D_2-\\mathbf D_1)=\\sigma_\\mathrm f,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf n\\times(\\mathbf E_2-\\mathbf E_1)=\\mathbf 0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The first condition follows from Gauss’s law\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∮<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\oint\\mathbf D\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf A=Q_\\mathrm{free}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a flat cylinder spanning the interface. Without free surface charge,\nthe normal component of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis continuous. The normal component of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan differ because of different values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second condition follows from the vanishing electrostatic\ncirculation of the electric field around a narrow loop crossing the\ninterface. Thus the tangential component of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis continuous. Polarisation charge is already included in the\ndescription through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>At the surface of an ideal conductor in electrostatic equilibrium,\nthe external field is normal to the surface, since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟎<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf E=\\mathbf 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninside. If the normal points out of the conductor,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{\\mathrm{outside},n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to the free surface charge density in the usual idealised\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Two layers in a parallel-plate capacitor<\/h2>\n<p>The plate area is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=40\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=12\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo layers of thickness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_1=d_2=0.50\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlie one behind the other in the field direction. Their relative\npermittivities are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm r,1}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm r,2}=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFringing fields and free charge at the common material interface are\nneglected. With the direction from the positive to the negative\nplate:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{1,n}=D_{2,n}=D_n,\\qquad\nU=E_{1,n}d_1+E_{2,n}d_2\n=D_n\\left(\\frac{d_1}{\\varepsilon_1}+\\frac{d_2}{\\varepsilon_2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{1,n}=2E_{2,n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage condition gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{1,n}=16\\,\\mathrm{kV/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{2,n}=8\\,\\mathrm{kV/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage drop across the first layer is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand across the second\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe magnitude of free plate charge is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=A D_n\\approx1.13\\,\\mathrm{nC}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe capacitance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>94.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=Q/U\\approx94.4\\,\\mathrm{pF}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe arrangement behaves like two capacitors in series because both\nlayers carry the same normal electric displacement flux.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_124_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two dielectric layers of equal thickness with electric field strengths of 16 and 8 kilovolts per metre, uniform normal electric flux density and piecewise linear potential.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: With equal normal electric flux density, the first\ndielectric layer has 16 kV/m field strength and the second 8 kV/m;\npotential remains continuous at the interface.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The lengths of the blue and green field arrows are in the ratio two\nto one. The lower graph shows the potential relative to the negative\nplate: it is continuous at the interface, but its slope changes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conductivity and model selection<\/h2>\n<p>Conductivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{S/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the ohmic transport of free charge. For an isotropic\nconductor, use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf J=\\kappa\\mathbf E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{A/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a straight, homogeneous conductor of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℓ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand cross-sectional area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na uniform field gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\ell/(\\kappa A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=0.10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=1\\,\\mathrm{mm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>7<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=5.8\\cdot10^7\\,\\mathrm{S/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.72<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\approx1.72\\,\\mathrm{m\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe model of real direct-current conduction has a small internal field.\nAn ideal dielectric has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbut can be polarised.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check and further study<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens if both layers in the example have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm r}=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which field condition remains satisfied when free surface charge is\nintroduced at the material interface?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solutions.<\/strong> 1. Both fields are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm{kV/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe arrangement is equivalent to one layer of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.00\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm r}=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>141.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\approx141.7\\,\\mathrm{pF}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a voltage drop of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper layer. 2. The tangential components of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremain equal. The normal component of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\njumps by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_\\mathrm f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen passing from medium 1 to medium 2.<\/p>\n<p>Permittivity and conductivity may depend on frequency, temperature\nand field strength. With time-dependent signals, losses and delays in\nthe material response play a role. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Magnetostatics\nand magnetic circuits<\/a> introduces the corresponding magnetic\nquantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-6-001-electromagnetic-fields-and-energy-spring-2008/pages/chapter-6/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Electromagnetic Fields and Energy, Chapter 6\nPolarization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§ 6.2–6.3 on electric flux density, boundary\nconditions and linear media. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-4-capacitor-with-a-dielectric\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 8.4 Capacitor with a Dielectric<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nCharge, voltage and capacitance with a dielectric. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-6-001-electromagnetic-fields-and-energy-spring-2008/pages/chapter-7/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Electromagnetic Fields and Energy, Chapter 7 Conduction\nand Electroquasistatic Charge Relaxation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 7.2 on steady ohmic\nconduction. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M4-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M4-L3 – Magnetostatics and Magnetic Circuits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Electromagnetic Fields and Waves<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Material\nproperties and boundary conditions<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Vector\ncalculus and spatial derivatives<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish magnetic field\nstrength, flux density and flux, apply Ampère’s law and calculate a\nsimple magnetic circuit with an air gap.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Magnetic field around a conductor<\/h2>\n<p>A time-independent current produces a magnetic field. For a long,\nstraight wire, the field lines run in circles around the axis of the\nwire. The right hand indicates their orientation. The thumb points in\nthe direction of conventional current, and the curled fingers indicate\nthe direction of circulation. In the magnetostatic case, if the\ndirection of circulation and the surface normal are defined by the same\nright-hand rule,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∮<\/mo><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\oint_{\\partial S}\\mathbf H\\cdot\\mathrm d\\boldsymbol{\\ell}\n=I_{\\mathrm f,S} .<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\mathrm f,S}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the free current through the oriented surface.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the magnetic field strength in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{A/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the magnetic flux density in tesla,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm T=1\\,\\mathrm{Wb/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a linear, isotropic material,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf B=\\mu\\mathbf H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=\\mu_0\\mu_\\mathrm r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.257<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_0\\approx1.257\\cdot10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{H/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe magnetic flux through an oriented surface is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi=\\int_S\\mathbf B\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf A .<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Only when the normal flux density is uniform does this simplify to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi=B_n A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe unit of flux is the weber. The total magnetic flux through every\nclosed surface is zero. Consequently, in the simple magnetic circuit\nevery portion of flux leaving the core returns through another\nsection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Simple field models<\/h2>\n<p>For a very long wire in air, cylindrical symmetry gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=I/(2\\pi r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutside the wire. For a long coil with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nturns and length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℓ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfar from its ends the internal field is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=(N/\\ell)I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTo obtain this result, choose an elongated closed contour with one side\ninside the nearly uniform coil field and the other in the weak external\nfield.<\/p>\n<p>Example of an air-core coil: The turn density is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe current is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the cross-sectional area is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=200\\,\\mathrm{A/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>251<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\approx251\\,\\mathrm{\\mu T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>50.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi\\approx50.3\\,\\mathrm{nWb}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper turn penetrated normally by the field in the uniform region.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equivalent model with a core and an air gap<\/h2>\n<p>A closed magnetic path with approximately uniform flux density across\nthe cross-sectional area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be divided into sections. Ampère’s law is approximated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i H_i\\ell_i=NI<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_i=B_i/\\mu_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand negligible leakage flux,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mfrac><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">NI=\\Phi\\sum_i\\frac{\\ell_i}{\\mu_i A_i}\n=\\Phi\\mathcal R_\\mathrm{m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The magnetic reluctance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal R_\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the unit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{A/Wb}=\\mathrm{H^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">NI<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the magnetomotive force. The equivalent model uses the same algebraic\nform as an electrical series circuit. Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes a flux of the magnetic flux density, not an electric\ncurrent.<\/p>\n<p>A core has a mean path length of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_\\mathrm r=2000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an air gap of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.50\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth cross-sectional areas are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.10\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>41.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.19<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\approx\n\\frac{\\mu_0 NI}{\\ell_\\mathrm K/\\mu_{\\mathrm r,K}+\\ell_\\mathrm g}\n=41.9\\,\\mathrm{mT},\n\\qquad\n\\Phi\\approx4.19\\,\\mathrm{\\mu Wb}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The core contributes an equivalent air length of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe gap therefore accounts for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5/6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the total reluctance. If all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nturns link the same flux, the inductance in the linear model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8.38<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=N\\Phi/I\\approx8.38\\,\\mathrm{mH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_125_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic core with winding and air gap, closed flux path and bars showing reluctance shares of one sixth for the core and five sixths for the gap.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Despite its short length, the air gap accounts for\nfive sixths of the reluctance in this simplified magnetic circuit;\nleakage flux and fringing are omitted.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The illustration is not to scale. Despite the short air gap, most of\nthe magnetic reluctance is concentrated there; leakage flux and fringing\nare neglected as in the calculation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check and further study<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the external field of a long wire change when the distance\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The air gap in the example is increased to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the new flux density and flux.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solutions.<\/strong> 1.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved, as is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the same surroundings. The direction of circulation remains the same\nif the current is unchanged. 2. The denominator grows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.60<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.10\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>22.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">22.8\\,\\mathrm{mT}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.28<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.28\\,\\mathrm{\\mu Wb}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>In real cores,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay vary with excitation. Saturation, hysteresis, leakage flux and\nfringing in the air gap limit the model. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Induction and\nelectromagnetic energy<\/a> covers the consequences of a change in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/12-5-amperes-law\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 12.5 Ampère’s Law<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 12.5 –\nAmpère’s law and the field around a long conductor. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-6-001-electromagnetic-fields-and-energy-spring-2008/d4860fdc85d667828060e6d72e49a62f_09.pdf\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – Electromagnetic Fields and Energy, Chapter 9\nMagnetization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 9.7 on the equivalent magnetic circuit. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M4-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M4-L4 – Induction and Electromagnetic Energy<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Electromagnetic Fields and Waves<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Magnetostatics\nand magnetic circuits<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Calculus and\ndifferential equations for time-dependent models<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine the induced\nelectromotive force of a stationary coil using a consistent orientation\nand explain energy transfer in the model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A changing flux produces an electromotive force<\/h2>\n<p>A stationary conducting loop in a constant magnetic field has no\nelectromotive force induced by that field. If the flux density within\nthe loop changes, however, an electric field can arise along the wire.\nIts circulation is generally non-zero. This distinguishes the case from\nelectrostatics, where all electric field components can be derived from\na scalar potential.<\/p>\n<p>Choose the surface normal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof a stationary loop. The positive direction of circulation around the\nboundary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthen follows from the right-hand rule. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm d\\mathbf A=\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm dA<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∮<\/mo><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi(t)=\\int_S\\mathbf B(\\mathbf r,t)\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf A,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal E=\\oint_{\\partial S}\\mathbf E\\cdot\\mathrm d\\boldsymbol\\ell\n=-\\frac{\\mathrm d\\Phi}{\\mathrm dt}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the induced electromotive force (EMF) in volts. Despite its\nname, it is energy per unit charge and does not have units of force. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequally oriented turns linking the same flux,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E=-N\\,\\mathrm d\\Phi/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lenz’s law as a sign check<\/h2>\n<p>If a positive flux increases,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a closed loop modelled as purely resistive, the current then flows\nopposite to the positive direction of circulation. Its magnetic field\nopposes the <strong>increase<\/strong> in the original flux. If the\npositive flux decreases, the induced current flows in the opposite\ndirection. It is therefore the change in flux that matters. Even a large\nbut constant field still gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin this model.<\/p>\n<p>Reversing the normal also reverses the positive direction of\ncirculation. The flux, EMF and calculated current then change sign. The\nphysical direction of current, determined by the current value\n<strong>and<\/strong> its associated reference direction, remains the\nsame.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example: a flux ramp<\/h2>\n<p>A stationary winding with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=6.0\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper turn is placed in a spatially uniform field. The normal flux density\nincreases uniformly from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>20<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{mT}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe modelled loop resistance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe consider the interior of the ramp interval and neglect\nself-inductance. In SI units:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.050<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>0.015<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>96<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot B_n=\\frac{0.050-0.020}{0.015}\\,\\mathrm{T/s}\n=2.0\\,\\mathrm{T/s},\n\\quad\n\\mathcal E=-N A\\dot B_n=-96\\,\\mathrm{mV},\n\\quad\ni=\\frac{\\mathcal E}{R}=-8.0\\,\\mathrm{mA}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The heat dissipated per unit time in the resistor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.768<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=Ri^2=0.768\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nApproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11.5\\,\\mathrm{\\mu J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis therefore dissipated during the ramp. An external device maintaining\nthe increasing field must supply energy to this coupled system. The\nnegative sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes its direction and does not produce negative power\ndissipation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_126_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Time plots of a flux-density ramp from 20 to 50 millitesla and the constant negative EMF of 96 millivolts within the ramp interval.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: During the idealised flux-density ramp from 20 to 50\nmT, a constant EMF of −96 mV is induced; the ramp endpoints are excluded\nfrom the model.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The EMF follows from the slope with respect to time, not the\ninstantaneous magnitude of the flux density. The open circles indicate\nthat the endpoints of the ramp are not treated in the simplified\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-induction and energy in the field<\/h2>\n<p>A winding generates its own flux as the current increases. For a\nlinear inductance, the flux linkage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Psi=Li<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe induced EMF is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\mathrm d\\Psi/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen the same component is written into a circuit using the\n<strong>passive sign convention<\/strong>, its terminal voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_L=L\\,\\mathrm di/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_L i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the power entering the component at its terminals:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>i<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>L<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>L<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_\\mathrm m=\\int_0^i L i&#39;\\,\\mathrm di&#39;=\\frac12 L i^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A lossless linear magnetic medium locally stores an energy density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_\\mathrm m=\\mathbf B\\cdot\\mathbf H/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{J/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a real winding, field energy storage and material losses must be\nconsidered separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check and further study<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the flux ramp,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecreases from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>50<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm{mT}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover the same period. How do the results change?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How much energy does a linear coil with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=8\\,\\mathrm{mH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstore at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=0.10\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solutions.<\/strong> 1.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>96<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E=+96\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>8.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=+8.0\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe quadratic power dissipation and energy remain unchanged. 2.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_\\mathrm m=40\\,\\mathrm{\\mu J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The example assumes a fixed loop geometry and a prescribed external\nfield. With rapid changes or large self-inductance, the winding and its\ncoupling influence the time response. In moving conductors, the magnetic\nforce on moving charge also contributes to the EMF. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Maxwell’s\nequations and wave propagation<\/a> summarises the mutual coupling of the\nfields.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-1-faradays-law\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 13.1 Faraday’s Law<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 13.1 –\nFlux change and induced EMF. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-2-lenzs-law\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 13.2 Lenz’s Law<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 13.2 –\nCurrent direction for increasing or decreasing flux. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-3-energy-in-a-magnetic-field\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 14.3 Energy in a Magnetic Field<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 14.3 – Inductance energy and field energy density. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M4-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M4-L5 – Maxwell's Equations and Wave Propagation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Electromagnetic Fields and Waves<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Vector\ncalculus and spatial derivatives<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Induction and\nelectromagnetic energy<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify the sources and curls\nof electromagnetic fields and determine the direction, propagation speed\nand power flow of a plane wave.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four equations and the material description<\/h2>\n<p>The differential form of Maxwell’s equations describes fields in\nspace and time. In macroscopic SI notation:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Equation<\/th>\n<th>Meaning<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf D=\\rho_\\mathrm f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Free charge density is the source of electric flux density.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>The net magnetic flux through any closed surface is zero.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\times\\mathbf E=-\\partial_t\\mathbf B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>A change in magnetic flux density produces a curl of the electric\nfield.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\times\\mathbf H=\\mathbf J_\\mathrm f+\\partial_t\\mathbf D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Free current and time-varying electric flux density contribute to\nthe curl of the magnetic field.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho_\\mathrm f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{C/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf J_\\mathrm f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{A/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t\\mathbf D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the same units as free current density. This term is called the\ndisplacement current density. Taking the divergence of the last equation\ngives zero on the left-hand side. Together with Gauss’s law for\nelectricity, this yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t\\rho_\\mathrm f+\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf J_\\mathrm f=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe change in free charge with time is thus linked to the free\ncurrent.<\/p>\n<p>Material relations are also required. In a linear, lossless,\nisotropic medium with constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon,\\mu&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf D=\\varepsilon\\mathbf E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf B=\\mu\\mathbf H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why a change propagates<\/h2>\n<p>Consider such a medium without free charge or free current. One model\ncase has fields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf E=E_x(z,t)\\mathbf e_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf H=H_y(z,t)\\mathbf e_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth are independent of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe curl equations yield:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_z E_x=-\\mu\\partial_t H_y,\n\\qquad\n\\partial_z H_y=-\\varepsilon\\partial_t E_x.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Differentiating the first equation with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand substituting the second gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_z^2 E_x=\\mu\\varepsilon\\partial_t^2 E_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor propagation in the positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirection, this wave equation has solutions of the form\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_x(z,t)=E_0\\cos(\\omega t-kz)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=\\omega/v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe speed is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=1/\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the wavelength is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=v/f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=\\omega/(2\\pi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a vacuum,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals the speed of light\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For the forward-travelling wave,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_y=E_x/\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere the wave impedance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=\\sqrt{\\mu/\\varepsilon}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin ohms. The field directions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf E,\\mathbf H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the propagation direction are mutually perpendicular. If the\npropagation direction is reversed while the electric polarisation\ndirection remains the same, the sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Power flow and example<\/h2>\n<p>The Poynting vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf S=\\mathbf E\\times\\mathbf H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the example it points in the positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirection. Averaged over one period,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle S_z\\rangle=E_0^2/(2\\eta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the <strong>peak value<\/strong>. The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the denominator comes from the time average of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=100\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=6.0\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a vacuum, using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\approx3.00\\cdot10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>377<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_0\\approx377\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe results are:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity<\/th>\n<th>Result<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wavelength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.00\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Peak magnetic field strength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15.9\\,\\mathrm{mA/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Peak magnetic flux density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20.0\\,\\mathrm{nT}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mean power flux density<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>47.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">47.7\\,\\mathrm{mW/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_127_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Spatial projection of a plane wave: electric field strength in the x direction and magnetic field strength in the y direction, both sinusoidal and in phase, with propagation and energy flow in the positive z direction.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The mutually perpendicular electric and magnetic\nfields are in phase; their cross product points to energy flow along\npositive z.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Both sinusoidal curves show the field components at the same instant,\neach normalised to its own peak value. They are not particle\ntrajectories. The axes are oriented so that the cross product of the\nelectric and magnetic fields points in the positive z direction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check and further study<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the same frequency in an ideal medium with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_\\mathrm r=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_\\mathrm r=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A forward-travelling wave has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf e_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\mathbf e_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn which direction does the energy flow?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solutions.<\/strong> 1.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=c/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\approx1.50\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>188.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=\\eta_0/2\\approx188.5\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n2.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf e_y\\times(-\\mathbf e_x)=\\mathbf e_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>A spatially infinite plane wave is a model. Finite sources can\nproduce curved wavefronts and complex near fields. Losses or\nfrequency-dependent material properties alter attenuation and phase\nbehaviour. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Waves at\nboundaries and on transmission lines<\/a> shows how a wave splits at an\ninterface.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/16-1-maxwells-equations-and-electromagnetic-waves\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 16.1 Maxwell’s Equations and\nElectromagnetic Waves<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 16.1 – Relationship between the four\nfield equations. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/16-2-plane-electromagnetic-waves\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 16.2 Plane Electromagnetic Waves<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 16.2 – Wave equation, field directions and wavelength. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/16-3-energy-carried-by-electromagnetic-waves\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 2, § 16.3 Energy Carried by Electromagnetic\nWaves<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 16.3 – Poynting vector and mean power flux density.\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M4-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M4-L6 – Waves at Boundaries and on Transmission Lines<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Electromagnetic Fields and Waves<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Maxwell’s\nequations and wave propagation<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sinusoidal\nalternating current and complex impedance<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate reflection\ncoefficients for normal wave incidence and for an idealised transmission\nline, distinguish field ratios from power ratios and interpret\npropagation delays.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A plane wave arrives at a boundary at normal incidence<\/h2>\n<p>A wave travels from medium 1 with wave impedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntowards a planar boundary with medium 2, which has wave impedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth media are assumed to be homogeneous and lossless. The electric\npolarisation is parallel to the interface. We denote the complex\namplitudes at the boundary, referenced to the same electric field\ndirection, by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the incident, reflected and transmitted waves.<\/p>\n<p>In the absence of an impressed surface current, the tangential fields\nare continuous across the boundary. For the backward-travelling wave,\nthe magnetic field has the opposite sign relative to the electric field.\nHence:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm i+E_\\mathrm r=E_\\mathrm t,\\qquad\n\\frac{E_\\mathrm i-E_\\mathrm r}{\\eta_1}=\\frac{E_\\mathrm t}{\\eta_2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Solving for the electric field amplitude ratios gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_E=\\frac{E_\\mathrm r}{E_\\mathrm i}\n=\\frac{\\eta_2-\\eta_1}{\\eta_2+\\eta_1},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{E_\\mathrm t}{E_\\mathrm i}=1+\\Gamma_E .<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The mean reflected power per unit area as a fraction of the incident\npower per unit area is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal R=|\\Gamma_E|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe transmitted power ratio, by contrast, is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒯<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal T=(\\eta_1/\\eta_2)|1+\\Gamma_E|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe impedance factor arises from the relationship between electric field\namplitude and power flow in the respective medium.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: air and an idealised dielectric<\/h2>\n<p>From air with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>377<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_1\\approx377\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe wave encounters a medium with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_\\mathrm r=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_\\mathrm r=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>188.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_2\\approx188.5\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe amplitude ratios are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_E=-1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm t/E_\\mathrm i=2/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the boundary, the reflected electric component is phase-shifted by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to the incident component. The power fractions are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal R=1/9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒯<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal T=8/9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir sum is one.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_012_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Normal incidence at a lossless boundary. The reflected electric field has a relative factor of −1/3. The transmitted field has a factor of 2/3.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: At normal incidence on an ideal lossless boundary,\nthe reflected electric-field amplitude has relative factor −1/3 and the\ntransmitted amplitude factor 2/3.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Voltage waves on a transmission line<\/h2>\n<p>An idealised, lossless two-conductor transmission line also carries\nforward- and backward-travelling waves. Its real characteristic\nimpedance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the ratio of voltage amplitude to current amplitude for a\n<strong>single<\/strong> forward-travelling wave. At the termination\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith the reference direction of current towards the load,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_L=U^++U^-,\n\\qquad\nI_L=\\frac{U^+-U^-}{Z_0},\n\\qquad\n\\Gamma_U=\\frac{U^-}{U^+}=\\frac{Z_L-Z_0}{Z_L+Z_0}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_L=Z_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nreflection at the load end vanishes. An ideal open circuit gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_U=+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_L=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an ideal short circuit,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_U=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_L=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth reflect all the incident power in the lossless model.<\/p>\n<p>A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nline with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_L=100\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreceives a forward-travelling voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npeak at the termination. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_U=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.333<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_L=1.333\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13.33<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_L=13.33\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare peak values. The mean load power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8.89<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_L^2/(2Z_L)\\approx8.89\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe forward-travelling wave carries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the backward-travelling wave about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.11<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.11\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference corresponds to the power in the load.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Propagation delay, self-check and further study<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=2.0\\cdot10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na change takes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto reach the load end. A reflection generated there reaches the source\nafter a further\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSteady-state phasors describe the settled sinusoidal response, but do\nnot themselves describe the initial switching wavefront. With fast\nedges, a line can be electrically long even at a low repetition\nfrequency. For a rough approximation as a lumped connection, its\npropagation delay must be much shorter than the characteristic signal\ntransition times.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What results at the end of the same\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nline for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_L=25\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the forward-travelling amplitude unchanged?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the round-trip propagation delay if the line length is\nhalved?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Solutions.<\/strong> 1.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_U=-1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.667<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_L\\approx0.667\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>26.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_L\\approx26.7\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe load absorbs about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.89<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8.89\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecause the reflected power fraction remains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n2. The total round-trip propagation delay is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For oblique incidence, polarisation is relevant to reflection. Real\ntransmission lines may exhibit attenuation, dispersion and additional\nmodes. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Back to\nthe study pathway<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://vtechworks.lib.vt.edu/bitstream/handle/10919/93253/Electromagnetics_Vol2_screen-reader-friendly.pdf\">Steven\nW. Ellingson – Electromagnetics, Volume 2 (2020, VT Publishing, PDF)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· § 5.1, pp. 56–59: normal incidence, boundary conditions and\nreflection. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://vtechworks.lib.vt.edu/bitstream/handle/10919/84164/Electromagnetics_Vol1_screen-reader-friendly.pdf\">Steven\nW. Ellingson – Electromagnetics, Volume 1 (2018, VT Publishing, PDF)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· § 3.12, pp. 47–48, Eq. 3.86: voltage reflection coefficient and line\ntermination. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
